{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310631,"nonce":"mZG/lHSgtM7l8fg+c0q1ezaCvL4=","title":"Compiler bug (OPEN, found 2026-06-21): a bitwise-op result (e.g. `bits \u0026 0x3FF`) stored in an INFERRED var does integer-truncating ...","message":"Found while writing the pure-Haxe f16→f32 decode for the Q4 dot kernel ([[project_haxe_q4_dot_phase3]]).\n\n`var mant = bits \u0026 0x3FF; mant / 1024.0` returns **0** (integer-truncated), not 0.333, even though `mant` prints as 341. BOTH `/floatconst` AND `*floatconst` truncate: `mant * 0.0009765625` also → 0.\n\n```\nvar a = bits \u0026 0x3FF;       a / 1024.0   // WRONG → 0       (inferred type from \u0026)\nvar b = bits + 0;           b / 1024.0   // OK    → 13.333   (inferred type from +)\nvar c:Int = bits \u0026 0x3FF;   c / 1024.0   // OK    → 0.333    (explicit :Int)\n                            a * 0.0009765625  // WRONG → 0\nvar d = 13653 \u0026 0x3FF;      d / 1024.0   // WRONG → 0        (const-folded \u0026 in main)\n```\nSo: the result of a **bitwise** op (`\u0026`) in an **inferred** var loses its Int-ness for subsequent float arithmetic (treated as int-truncating), while `+`'s inferred result keeps it, and an explicit `:Int` annotation always fixes it. Likely the inferred type of `bits \u0026 0x3FF` (the `\u0026` result) is mis-tracked (narrow int-literal type from `0x3FF`? not a clean Int) so the float-arith lowering picks integer semantics. The static type, not the runtime value, drives it (value is correct = 341).\n\nAnnotate every bitwise-derived int that feeds float math as `:Int`. f16ToF32 with `var sign:Int=…; var exp:Int=…; var mant:Int=…` produces correct values (1.0/2.0/0.5/-2.0/0x3555→0.333251953125/0x0001→5.96e-8/0x2E66→0.0999755859375). The Q4 kernel's `q4_k_get_scale_min` (6-bit unpack via `\u0026 0x3F`, `\u003e\u003e6`, `\u003c\u003c4`) and f16 decode must use `:Int` on every intermediate.\n\nMake the inferred result type of a bitwise BinaryOp be Int (same as `+`/`-`), so `/`·`*` with a Float promote to float division. Check tast type inference for `\u0026`/`|`/`^`/`\u003c\u003c`/`\u003e\u003e` result types vs the arithmetic ops. Likely a one-spot fix in the type checker's binary-op result-type rule. NOT yet root-caused; only worked around. General bug — bites any bit-manipulation + float (fixed-point, hashing-to-float, quant).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_inferred_int_bitwise_float_div` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}